  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 227 PM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows a trough axis across \ncentral IL to the northern parts of Central Indiana. The convective \ncomplex has advanced to Central IL. Radar shows a few scattered \nthunderstorms ahead of this line pushing across eastern IL. Dew \npoints have surged to the 70s, but temperatures have been a bit slow \nto rise due to cloud cover.\n\nTonight...\n\nUncertainty exist with he progression of the thunderstorm cluster \nover IL. This system appears to be moving in to a less favorable \nenvironment, due to morning clouds, but with the afternoon heating, \nthe environment is becoming more favorable for continued progression \nto the ESE.\n\nThe continued NW flow aloft and another short wave are expect \novernight. This will generate showers and thunderstorm once again \novernight. Models show favorable isentropic lift setting up ahead of \nthe short wave overnight particularly along and north of LAF to \nRushville Axis. Given our very high dew points showers and storms \nwill be efficient rain producers and 1-2 inches of rain with \nisolated higher amounts cannot be ruled out. Forecast soundings show \na saturated column with very high pwats over 2.2 inches overnight. \nAdditional rainfall over already flooded area may worsen the \nsituation. Thus will be using high pops tonight, focusing the best \ntiming from mid evening through 08Z.  \n\nFriday and Friday Night...\n\nModels continue to suggest ridging riding forcing dynamics \ncontinuing to pass across Central Indiana on Friday, with the \nfavorable air mass still in place along with the trough axis slowly \nmoving south across Indiana. Forecast soundings on Friday remain \nsaturated with very pwats still above 2 inches as lift and \ninstability remain in place. Thus will continue high pops through \nthe day again on Friday and Friday night, with best chances to \nremain along and near and I-74 corridor.\n\nSaturday and Saturday Night...\n\nForecast soundings are shown to dry out on Saturday and Saturday \nnight as convective temperatures become unreachable. However a very \nwarm and humid air mass will remain in place which may result in \nneed for a heat advisory as mid 90s will be possible. Models hint \nthat the best forcing path will push to the north and more limited \nembedded waves look to pass. Thus should there be any diurnal \nconvection, best chances would be across the already soaked northern \nparts of the forecast area.\n\nSunday and Sunday Night...\n\nRain chances look to persist on Sunday as a cold front will push \nacross Central Indiana though the afternoon and into the evening. \nUpper levels suggest a continued WNW flow with a weak short wave \npassing through the afternoon to provide forcing. Warm and humid air \nwill be in place ahead of this system. Thus plenty of factors in \nplay to support pops. Cold front is suggested to sag southward to \nthe Ohio River by late Sunday Night and precip should end near that \ntime.\n\nMonday through Thursday...\n\nModels suggest continue WNW flow in place aloft during this time, \nbut little in the way of forcing dynamics appear to be in play. \nMeanwhile during this time, cooler high pressure in the wake of \nSunday's cold front will be pushing across Indiana and through the \nregion. This will lead to lower dewpoints and cooler highs. Forecast \nsoundings show a dry column through Wednesday morning as these \nfeatures pass.\n\nOn Wednesday afternoon and into Thursday the surface high will be \ndeparting to the southeast. This will allow another surface boundary \nover the Great Lakes to sag southward across Indiana at this time. \nBy this time, southerly return flow will have allowed warmer and \nmore humid air to arrive across Central Indiana. Furthermore, the \nflow aloft looks to become more NW as ridging over the western US \nbecomes more amplified. Although the signal for embedded waves \nwithin this flow is unclear at this time, given the pattern, \nembedded waves providing forcing appears plausible. Thus pops and \nwarmer temperatures will be used.",
